Simple Definition (Easy to Remember):
A set is a collection of different objects or items that are grouped together.
Examples
A = {1, 2, 3, 4} → A set of numbers.
B = {a, e, i, o, u} → A set of vowels.
C = {Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday} → A set of days.

Important Points
The objects in a set are called elements.
Elements are written inside curly braces { }.
Each element is written only once (no repetition).
The order of elements does not matter.

Example
If A = {2, 4, 6, 8}, then:
2, 4, 6, and 8 are the elements of set A.
We write 2 ∈ A (2 belongs to A).
We write 5 ∉ A (5 does not belong to A).

Notation of Set
Definition:
Set notation is the standard way of writing a set using capital letters and curly braces { }.

Rules of Set Notation
Sets are usually represented by capital letters such as A, B, C, X, Y.
Elements of a set are written inside curly braces { }.
Elements are separated by commas (,).
Each element is written only once.

Examples
A = {1, 2, 3, 4}
B = {a, e, i, o, u}
C = {Red, Blue, Green}

Representation of Sets
Definition:
The representation of a set means the method of writing or describing the elements of a set.
There are two main methods of representing a set:

1. Roster (Tabular/List) Form
In this method, all the elements of the set are listed inside curly braces { }, separated by commas.
Examples:
A = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
B = {a, e, i, o, u}
C = {Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday}
Advantage: Easy to understand for small sets.

2. Set-Builder (Rule) Form
In this method, a rule or property is used to describe the elements of the set.
Examples:
A = {x | x is an even natural number less than 12}
B = {x | x is a vowel in the English alphabet}
C = {x | x is a prime number less than 10}
Here, the symbol | means "such that."

